const schema = [{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"Service","name":"Medico-Legal Support Services","serviceType":"Medical record review and litigation support","provider":{"@type":"Organization","name":"Pubrica","url":"https://pubrica.com/"},"url":"https://pubrica.com/services/medico-legal-support-services/","description":"Medical record review, timed clinical chronology, breach and causation analysis and report drafting — against the standard of the governing jurisdiction.","areaServed":"Worldwide","audience":{"@type":"Audience","audienceType":"Researchers, PhD scholars, research institutions, medical writers and life-science teams"}},{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"Are you the expert witness, or the people who help the expert?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The second, and the distinction is the most important thing on this page. We are a medical record review, chronology, literature and drafting service. Where an opinion on the medicine is required, it is given by a clinician practising in the relevant specialty who signs it in their own name — and whose duty, under CPR Part 35, is to help the court on matters within their expertise, a duty that “overrides any obligation to the person from whom experts have received instructions or by whom they are paid”. We prepare the material that opinion rests on: the reconciled bundle, the timed chronology, the contemporaneous guidance, the literature with its search log, and the adverse-facts schedule. We do not write an expert’s opinion for them, and a supplier who offers to is offering something that will not survive cross-examination about how the report came to be written."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What is the standard of care, and is it international?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No, and it is worth saying why not. “Standard of care analysis aligned with international guidelines” is offered widely in this market, and there is no international standard of care. It is set by the law of the jurisdiction and, in practice, by what a responsible body of practitioners there would have done at the time — and it is usually the contested issue in the claim. In England and Wales that is Bolam, as qualified by Bolitho: a practice accepted as proper by a responsible body of medical opinion, provided that body’s view has a logical basis and its proponents weighed the comparative risks and benefits. In the United States it is set state by state, with most states applying a national professional standard for specialists. In India the line runs through Jacob Mathew. A guideline from another country can be persuasive supporting evidence and it does not set the standard — and a report that leans on it in place of the governing standard is weaker, not stronger. Opposing counsel will make that point and they will be right."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Which Indian regulator governs this work?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Not the one still named on a great many pages, and on material sold for legal and regulatory accuracy that matters. The Medical Council of India no longer exists. It was replaced by the National Medical Commission, established under the National Medical Commission Act 2019 — the NMC’s own website refers throughout to the “erstwhile MCI”. Pages naming the MCI among the regulators they work to, or hedging with “MCI/NMC”, are citing a body that no longer exists — and hedging is worse than choosing either. Two related points: section 43A of the Information Technology Act 2000 is no longer the live Indian data-protection basis, because India has enacted the Digital Personal Data Protection Act 2023 — we were not able to confirm that Act’s current commencement and rules position, so rather than assert a status we have not checked, we confirm it at the start of any Indian instruction. And “Level A evidence” cited with no framework named is ambiguous rather than wrong — but ambiguity in a document that gets cross-examined is a defect either way."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Does Bolam apply to consent?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No, and this is the most common error we find in older medico-legal reports. Montgomery v Lanarkshire Health Board [2015] UKSC 11 — a Scottish appeal binding across the UK — replaced the professional-practice test for information disclosure with a patient-centred one. The doctor must take reasonable care to ensure the patient is aware of any material risk in the recommended treatment and of any reasonable alternatives, with materiality judged by what a reasonable person in the patient’s position would attach significance to, or what the doctor is or should reasonably be aware this particular patient would attach significance to. So a consent analysis measured against what a responsible body of doctors would have disclosed is applying a test that has not been the law for over a decade — and the defence will identify it in a paragraph. Where consent is in issue the analysis is built on Montgomery and the records are read for what the patient was actually told, by whom and when, which is frequently the part of the record that is thinnest."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What must a report going to court contain?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In England and Wales, CPR Part 35 and Practice Direction 35 govern. Rule 35.3 sets the overriding duty to help the court, which displaces any obligation to whoever instructed or paid. PD 35 requires the evidence to be “the independent product of the expert uninfluenced by the pressures of litigation”; that the expert not assume the role of advocate; that they consider all material facts, including those which might detract from their opinion; that they make clear when a question falls outside their expertise and when they cannot reach a definite view; and that any material change of opinion be communicated without delay. Rule 35.10 requires the report to state the substance of all material instructions, written or oral, and that material is not protected from disclosure in the ordinary way. The report ends with the statement of truth at PD 35 paragraph 3.3. The “all material facts” requirement is the one most often breached, and it is breached by omission rather than by anything deliberate — which is why the adverse-facts schedule is part of what we deliver. Other courts have other rules, and a report drafted to the wrong rulebook is challenged on its form before anyone argues about its substance."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What is different about a United States instruction?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The state is the first question, not a detail. The standard of care is set state by state; most states apply a national professional standard for specialists while some retain or modify a locality rule. Many states require an affidavit or certificate of merit from a qualified expert before a malpractice claim may proceed at all, with the qualifying criteria and the deadline set by statute — a claim can be dismissed on that alone, before anyone reaches the medicine. Admissibility in federal court is governed by Federal Rule of Evidence 702, amended with effect from 1 December 2023 to make explicit that the proponent must show admissibility is more likely than not, and that the opinion reflects a reliable application of the methodology to the facts — addressing courts that had been treating overstatement as going to weight rather than admissibility. Some states apply the older Frye general-acceptance test instead. We draft to the rules of the court the report is going to, and we ask which court before we start."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How quickly can we get a review?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"A screening review of up to 300 pages in 5 business days; a full records review and chronology to 1,000 pages in 10; breach and causation analysis in 15; drafting support for a report to the court in 20. Turnaround in this market is usually given as prose — basic consultations “may take a few hours”, comprehensive reports “could take several days” — which is not something a buyer working to a court deadline can plan around. Two things move a real timeline more than page count. Missing records, which is why the reconciliation happens first and the schedule reaches you on the day it is produced. And a clinician’s calendar where an opinion is needed, which is a person rather than a process step. Expedited work is available where capacity allows and is quoted on capacity rather than on urgency; where a date is not achievable we say so rather than taking the instruction and missing it."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How is patient data handled?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"As special category health data, which is what it is. A non-disclosure agreement is signed before any material moves. Records are held under the UK GDPR and the Data Protection Act 2018 on access-controlled systems within a quality management system certified to ISO 9001:2015 — certificate H3CR9TCU20251212INDQM10N — with access limited to the named reviewers on the matter, retention for the period the instruction requires, secure deletion on your instruction, and no use in training anything. Where an instruction touches another regime — HIPAA in the United States, India’s Digital Personal Data Protection Act 2023 — we confirm the current position at the start of that instruction rather than relying on what was true at the last one. We will also work entirely inside your own document management system where you require it; several of our clients do, and it is a reasonable thing to require."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Will you tell us if the case is weak?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes, and that is most of what you are buying. The sample review on this page concluded that causation was not made out on the records then available — against the interest of the party who instructed it — and then named the three documents that would change that, two of which arrived. A review that tells an instructing solicitor only what they hoped is a review they cannot rely on, and an expert who cannot concede anything is not believed on the points where they are right. Two related commitments. We schedule the material facts that cut against the instructing party, whichever side instructs, because PD 35 requires an expert to consider all material facts including those detracting from their opinion, and because you want them before the other side has them. And we do not promise a “strong case”, however often it is offered elsewhere: strength is a judgement for the instructing lawyer, made on material a record reviewer does not hold. Defensible is the word, and it was already the right one elsewhere on the same page."}}]},{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"BreadcrumbList","itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Home","item":"https://pubrica.com/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"Services","item":"https://pubrica.com/services/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"name":"Services","item":"https://pubrica.com/services/medico-legal-support-services/"}]}];

